Subject: [PATCH 5/7] v8: range-check folding pass ((x>=lo && x<=hi) ->
 (unsigned)(x-lo) <= hi-lo)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

C's a && b / a || b lower to short-circuit control flow which if-conversion
turns into Select chains. When both arms compare the SAME value against
constant bounds, the whole boolean dance (two cmp+setcc+movzbl, a test,
a cmov, a narrowing cast) collapses into one subtract and one unsigned
compare — exactly GCC's shape for Expat's xml_name_continue / SQLite's
varint classifier.

Folds, for lo <= hi:
  x >= lo && x <= hi  =>  (unsigned)(x - lo) <= (hi - lo)
  x <  lo || x >  hi  =>  (unsigned)(x - lo) >  (hi - lo)
Wraparound subtraction makes out-of-range values wrap huge, so a single
unsigned compare classifies the range; valid for signed and unsigned
comparisons in any width provided hi-lo is representable. The two arms
are matched through their cast chains (the lowering casts the operand
once per comparison), and the boolean result keeps the Select's type so
every downstream use is unchanged. Runs right after if-conversion.

Measured on the corpus (paired, pinned): expat 3.28x -> 3.13x,
hash_table 1.38x -> 1.31x, loop_patterns 1.85x -> 1.80x; no regressions.
Regression 127 passed / 0 failed, correctness 50/50, intrinsics 3/3,
differential range-check test bit-exact vs gcc over all byte values and
INT/LLONG edge cases.
